#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# The line above MUST be the very first line of the file (byte-for-byte) —
# it's called a "shebang" and only works as the file's first two
# characters (`#!`). It tells the operating system which program should
# interpret the rest of the file when you run it directly (e.g.
# `./bin/brakeman`). `/usr/bin/env ruby` means "find whatever `ruby` is
# first on the system's PATH and use that to run this file" — this is
# more portable than hard-coding a path like `/usr/bin/ruby`, since Ruby
# may be installed in different places on different machines (e.g. via a
# version manager like rbenv or mise). Without this line, the shell
# wouldn't know this text file is meant to be run as a Ruby program.
# `require` loads a Ruby library (a "gem" or standard library file) so its
# code becomes available to use below. "rubygems" is Ruby's built-in
# package manager library — loading it here ensures the `Gem` module
# (used a few lines down) is available even on Ruby setups where it isn't
# auto-loaded.
require "rubygems"
# Loads Bundler's setup code. Bundler is the tool that manages this app's
# gem dependencies (listed in the Gemfile/Gemfile.lock). Requiring
# "bundler/setup" makes Ruby's `require` calls only see the exact gem
# versions locked in Gemfile.lock, rather than whatever gem versions
# happen to be installed system-wide — this avoids "works on my machine"
# version mismatches.
require "bundler/setup"

# Blank line — just visual separation between the "load the environment"
# section above and the "configure and run the tool" section below.

# `ARGV` is a built-in Ruby array holding the command-line arguments this
# script was called with (e.g. running `bin/brakeman -q` gives ARGV =
# ["-q"]). `.unshift(x)` adds `x` to the FRONT of that array. So this line
# forces the `--ensure-latest` flag onto every invocation of Brakeman,
# regardless of what the user typed — Brakeman is a security scanner for
# Rails apps, and this flag makes it check it's running the newest release
# before scanning, so security checks don't silently go stale.
ARGV.unshift("--ensure-latest")

# Blank line for visual separation before the final "hand off control" step.

# This is the core pattern used by every Bundler-generated bin/ wrapper
# script in this app (see also bin/rubocop, bin/thrust, bin/kamal).
# `Gem.bin_path("brakeman", "brakeman")` asks RubyGems: "find the
# executable file named 'brakeman' that belongs to the installed gem also
# named 'brakeman', and give me its full file path." `load` then reads
# that file and runs it, as if its code were pasted right here. Why not
# just run the `brakeman` command directly from the terminal? Because that
# would use whatever `brakeman` happens to be first on the system PATH —
# possibly a different version than the one locked in this app's
# Gemfile.lock. Going through `bundler/setup` (required above) plus
# `Gem.bin_path` guarantees this script always runs the EXACT gem version
# this project depends on, even if other versions are installed elsewhere
# on the machine.
load Gem.bin_path("brakeman", "brakeman")
